The {{nihongo|'''Tokuyama Line'''|徳山線|Tokuyama-sen}} is a [[rapid transit]] line operated by [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]] in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. Designated as '''Line 6''' of the Okami Metro network, it is distinguished by its purple color ({{color box|#910f91|border=darkgray}}) and station prefix '''K'''. The line runs diagonally from [[Tokai]] in the northwest to [[Hikari-cho]] in the southeast, spanning {{cvt|26.9|km|mi|abbr=on}} ({{cvt|26.04|km|mi|abbr=on}} in GIS route alignment) and serving 21 stations across the [[Nishi]], [[Sugamo]], [[Aoi]], [[Chiyoda]], [[Chuo]], [[Tamade]], [[Higashi]], [[Tobe]], and [[Masaki]] wards and municipalities.

The Tokuyama Line is one of three lines in the Okami Metro network that operates 10-car [[Okami Metro 5000 series|5000 series]] trains classified as '''Type A''', the largest rolling stock type in the system. Constructed to {{RailGauge|1067mm|allk=on}} narrow gauge with {{1,500 V DC}} [[overhead catenary]] electrification, the line is engineered for reciprocal [[through-service]] operations with [[Okaiken Railways]] (OR) at both ends via the OR Tokuyama Line North Connector (at [[Mitani]] toward [[Imaichi-Meimon]] on the [[OR Iwashi Line]]) and the OR Tokuyama Line Metro Connector (at [[Hikari-cho]] onto the [[OR Main Line]]).

The line serves 11 major interchange hubs, including connections to the [[OR Iwashi Line]] at [[Makoya]], the [[Sanshi Main Line]] and OR Tokuyama Line North Connector at [[Mitani]], the {{OMSN|H|27}} [[Hitotsubashi Line]] at [[Teruo]], the {{OMSN|I|12}} [[Imaichi Line]] at [[Hanazono]], the {{OMSN|S|13}} [[Shitayanagi Line]] and {{OMSN|G|20}} [[Tokushige Line]] at [[Yamabe]], the {{OMSN|C|22}} [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]] at [[Hamakura]], the {{OMSN|B|20}} [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]] at [[Suminoe]], the {{OMSN|M|28}} [[Miyoshi Line]] and [[Tokuden Sakae Line]] at [[Yugawara]], the [[Tokuden Main Line]] and [[Toukai Main Line]] at [[Tamade]], the {{OMSN|T|45}} [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]] at [[Yuki Daigaku]] and {{OMSN|T|56}} [[Higashi Shigemachi]], the {{OMSN|N|22}} [[Nagata Line]] at [[Kuninushi]], the {{OMSN|A|16}} [[Asahi Line]] at [[Shigemachi]], and the [[OR Main Line]] at [[Hikari-cho]].

==History==

===Planning and construction===
The Tokuyama Line was planned concurrently with the [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]] in the early 1960s as part of the second major expansion phase of the Okami Municipal Subway. The route was designed to serve a major diagonal commercial and residential corridor linking the northwestern districts through the central wards to the southeastern municipalities of [[Tobe]] and [[Masaki]]. To enable direct through-running onto the [[Okaiken Railways]] commuter network without gauge-changing equipment, the line was built to {{RailGauge|1067mm|allk=on}} narrow gauge with 1,500 V DC overhead catenary and 10-car platform lengths.

Construction began in 1965, with the line sharing deep-level engineering standards with the Nanboku Line in the central Chiyoda ward area. The diagonal alignment required careful planning to avoid conflicts with existing underground utilities and historic foundations in the Aoi and Chiyoda districts.

===Opening and early operations===
The Tokuyama Line opened on October 1, 1969, the same year as the Nanboku Line. The initial segment operated between [[Tokai]] and [[Hamakura]], with the southeastern extension through [[Tamade]], [[Higashi]], and [[Tobe]] to [[Hikari-cho]] completed in stages over the following decade. The initial fleet consisted of 10-car [[Okami Metro 1000 series|1000 series]] formations built to Type A loading gauge to accommodate heavy peak-hour commuter flows.

===Modernization and through-service expansion===
On April 1, 2018, as part of the privatization of the Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau, the Tokuyama Line was transferred to [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]] The legacy 1000 series fleet was retired and replaced by 10-car [[Okami Metro 5000 series|5000 series]] Type A trains manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]], introducing regenerative braking, higher acceleration up to {{cvt|100|km/h|mph|abbr=on}}, and integration with the Satori-AI passenger management system. Station numberings from {{OMSN|K|01}} to {{OMSN|K|21}} and full platform screen doors were also commissioned across all 21 stations, and station K14 (formerly Uramachi) was designated {{nihongo|Yuki Daigaku|由紀大学前}} to serve the adjacent Yuki University campus.

