==Rolling stock==

The Tokuyama Line operates 10-car '''Type A''' formations ({{cvt|200|m|ft|abbr=on}} long, {{cvt|3.0|m|ft|abbr=on}} wide) built to {{RailGauge|1067mm|allk=on}} narrow gauge with 1,500 V DC overhead catenary collection:

* '''[[Okami Metro 5000 series|5000 series]]''' (10-car Type A sets, 2018–present): Manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]] and introduced following privatization to replace the 1000 series. Equipped with dual-system signalling for through-service onto [[Okaiken Railways]] corridors and full Satori-AI cabin and crowd-management telemetry.
* '''[[Okami Metro 1000 series|1000 series]]''' (retired): Operated on the Tokuyama Line from its opening in 1969 until March 2018.

==Through-service==

Because the Tokuyama Line was constructed to {{RailGauge|1067mm|allk=on}} narrow gauge with 10-car Type A platforms, it connects directly to the [[Okaiken Railways]] (OR) regional network via two dedicated physical track links recorded in the metropolitan rail network:

* '''OR Tokuyama Line North Connector''': Links the northwestern section of the Tokuyama Line at [[Mitani]] ({{OMSN|K|03}}) via [[Tedori Meimon]] ({{OMSN|G|14}}) to [[Imaichi-Meimon]] (W04) on the [[OR Iwashi Line]], enabling through-running between northwestern OR commuter services and central Okami.
* '''OR Tokuyama Line Metro Connector''': A {{cvt|0.44|km|mi|abbr=on}} southern connecting track at [[Hikari-cho]] ({{OMSN|K|21}}) that links the southeastern terminus of the Tokuyama Line directly to Hikari-cho Station (O16) on the [[OR Main Line]] in Masaki, allowing Tokuyama Line trains to continue southward toward [[Tokuyama Prefecture]].

